# Compiled Python extensions and their debug symbols.
# maturin writes these into the SOURCE tree (cleargbm_rs sets
# python-source = "python", so `maturin develop` drops the artifacts next to
# __init__.py, which build/ and target/ do not cover). They must not be
# committed: cleargbm_rs builds with -C target-cpu=native, so the binary is
# not portable across CPU generations, and every consumer installs from the
# maturin wheel or a develop path-dep rather than from these files.
*.pyd
*.so
*.pdb

# Logs
logs/
*.log

# OS/IDE
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Caches
.cache/
.python-version

# Claude Code
.claude/

# Gemini
.gemini/

# Data directories (each service manages its own)
**/data/
# Allow the TankpitBot per-colour tank registry to be versioned: it is
# measured state (each account's rank per colour per world), useless if
# a clean of ignored files takes it -- which is how its neighbour the
# equipment atlas disappeared mid-run on 2026-08-28. Re-ignore the
# directory's contents so only this one file escapes the rule.
!clients/TankpitBot/data/
clients/TankpitBot/data/*
!clients/TankpitBot/data/tank_registry.json

# Allow covenant-radar-api external datasets to be versioned for tests/benchmarks
!services/covenant-radar-api/data/
!services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/
!services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/**
# Exclude large Kaggle datasets (>50MB, use kaggle CLI to download)
# rw_matches is locally generated rather than downloaded -- the RustedWarfare
# match fleet writes it -- but data.csv is 64MB, over the same threshold, and
# it is reproducible by re-running matches. Same rule, different source.
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/rw_matches/
# rw_value derives deterministically from rw_matches (covenant_ml's
# scripts/derive_rw_value.py) and is the same size class. Same rule.
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/rw_value/
# weather_tmax raw GHCN-D station files (~50MB total) are pinned by
# sha256 in raw/MANIFEST.json (committed) and refetchable by
# fetch_ghcnd_weather.py; the derived data.csv IS committed.
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/weather_tmax/raw/*.csv.gz
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/kaggle_amex_default/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/kaggle_lending_club/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/kaggle_home_credit/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/kaggle_credit_card_fraud/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/kaggle_sba_loans/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/kaggle_risk_analytics/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/kaggle_prosper_loans/

# THREE GITHUB-SOURCED DATASETS THAT WERE NEVER ACTUALLY COMMITTED, and one
# reference clone at the repo root. All four were recorded as GITLINKS -- bare
# submodule pointers -- because each still contained a `.git` directory when it
# was added, so `git add` stored a commit id instead of the files. There is no
# `.gitmodules` in this repository, so those pointers name a commit nothing can
# fetch: a fresh clone gets four empty directories, and every CI run since has
# warned `fatal: No url found for submodule path 'amex_1st_place'` followed by
# `The process 'git' failed with exit code 128`.
#
# They are ignored rather than committed. Two reasons, and the second is the
# real one:
#   * three of them no longer even contain a `.git`, so nothing here is a
#     submodule any more -- the pointer is a fossil;
#   * they are third-party research artifacts published with their own papers
#     (PAKDD 2021 for the HAT set, Information Sciences 2024 for ComRisk), and
#     redistributing 44 MB of somebody else's dataset from a PUBLIC repository
#     is a licensing decision, not a cleanup. Committing them is still open --
#     the `!.../data/external/**` rule above says the intent was to version
#     them -- but it needs an owner who has read those licences.
#
# The commit each pointer named, kept here because removing the gitlinks is
# what deletes it from the tree:
#   amex_1st_place                 03b86f584bb61a34e31eb6343bd33ae753a92c02
#     from https://github.com/jxzly/Kaggle-American-Express-Default-Prediction-1st-solution.git
#   github_american_bankruptcy     8bcd8db3b432b1fa6d65e26753b5c9fc6567438d
#   github_chinese_hat             843eb0a30059581e9153662f8544453ec1506a86
#   github_chinese_smesd           a80524b3b67436cd2f74755f6ffa08a554ff2d02
# The last three lost their `.git` before this was found, so their upstream
# URLs are not recoverable from the checkout; each carries a README naming the
# paper it accompanies.
/amex_1st_place/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/github_american_bankruptcy/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/github_chinese_hat/
services/covenant-radar-api/data/external/github_chinese_smesd/
**/datasets/
# But allow source code directories named "datasets"
!**/src/**/datasets/
!**/tests/**/datasets/
**/outputs/
**/checkpoints/
**/corpus_cache/
**/artifacts/
**/runs/
# But an hpc3 workspace document is configuration, not a result. It declares a
# project's partition, wall clock, image digest and cluster root -- a clone
# without it has no projects at all and every campaign refuses. Four of them
# survive here only because they were committed before this rule existed, and
# git ignores untracked files rather than tracked ones; the fifth (rusted) was
# silently never committed. Re-including the directory and then its contents
# is the only shape that works: git will not descend into an excluded
# directory, so a bare negation of the files alone has no effect.
!tools/hpc3/runs/
tools/hpc3/runs/*
!tools/hpc3/runs/hpc3*.json
# Corpus digest records are provenance, not run output: they are what a
# cluster-side stage verifies against. Two were already tracked only
# because someone force-added them, which is the shape the note above
# warns about — the next one would have gone missing silently.
!tools/hpc3/runs/*-digests.txt
# Stage manifests are the other half of that provenance: the digests record
# says which bytes the work published, and the manifest says where they were
# put and why that selection. Committing one and ignoring the other leaves a
# record that can prove a file arrived intact and cannot say what it is.
#
# Named `*-stage.json` rather than force-added, which is what the first pair
# of these got until it was noticed. A negation is the fix and a `git add -f`
# is not: forcing works for whoever runs it and silently drops the file for
# the next person, which is exactly the failure the note above records.
!tools/hpc3/runs/*-stage.json
# A run document and the payload it names are configuration too, and by the
# same argument: `modeltrainer-cluster-train --payload P` and
# `modeltrainer-continuations --spec S` are unrunnable without them, so a
# clone carrying the run document and not the payload carries a command that
# cannot start. `mi-arm-a.json` and `mi-arm-b.json` survive as history, the
# shape the note above warns about; these are a negation instead.
#
# Force-added first, in 5bea978c, and corrected here for the reason dead545b
# gives one commit earlier: a force works for whoever runs it and silently
# drops the file for the next person.
!tools/hpc3/runs/code-style-*.json
# Same shape again, for the cartridge measurement: the run document that pins
# an image digest and the corpora it reads is configuration, and a clone
# without it cannot reproduce the A100 record.
#
# A per-body-of-work prefix rather than a blanket `!*.json`, because 249 of the
# 439 files in this directory are untracked run documents from past work. A
# blanket negation would propose committing all of them, and the ignore is
# doing real work by holding them back.
#
# That number is itself worth reading: run documents going missing is not a
# hypothetical this rule prevents, it is the steady state everywhere a prefix
# has not been negated.
!tools/hpc3/runs/cartridge-*.json
**/corpus/

# But allow source code directories named "data"
!**/src/**/data/
!**/tests/**/data/

